====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|slang|dated|intransitive}} To [[talk]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1912|author={{w|Jack London}}|title=Smoke Bellew|url=https://archive.org/details/smokebellew00lond|chapter=5|page=141|publisher=Grosset & Dunlap|location=New York
|passage=“I reckon you can explain, Mrs. Peabody.” {{...}} “An’ I reckon that newcomer you’ve been '''chinning''' with could explain if ''he'' had a mind to.”}}
#* '''1944''', {{w|Ernie Pyle}}, ''Brave Men'', New York: Henry Holt, Chapter 1, p.{{nbsp}}3,<sup>[https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20171115/html.php]</sup>
#*: This little chore involved getting up at 3 <small>A</small>.<small>M</small>., working about two hours, then sitting around '''chinning''' and drinking coffee with the radio operators until too late to go back to sleep.
# {{lb|en|slang|dated|transitive}} To talk to or with (someone).
#* '''1911''', {{w|Henry Sydnor Harrison}}, ''Queed'', Boston: Houghton Mifflin, Chapter 7, p.{{nbsp}}85,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/cu31924022462729]</sup>
#*: “Been up '''chinning''' your sporting editor, Ragsy Hurd. {{...}}”
#* '''1912''', {{w|Nancy Mann Waddel Woodrow}}, ''The Black Pearl'', New York: Appleton, Chapter 12, p.{{nbsp}}239,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/theblackpearl00woodrich]</sup>
#*: “What do you suppose that Seagreave’s '''chinning''' Hughie about[?]”
# {{lb|en|reflexive|intransitive}} To perform a [[chin-up]] {{gloss|exercise in which one lifts one's own weight while hanging from a bar}}.
#* '''1913''', {{w|Upton Sinclair}}, ''The Fasting Cure'', New York: Mitchell Kennerley, p.{{nbsp}}34,<sup>[https://archive.org/stream/fastingcure00sincuoft]</sup>
#*: It is worth noting that on the eighth day he was strong enough to '''“chin” himself''' six times in succession, though previous to the fasting treatment he had never in his life been able to do this more than once or twice.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1922|author={{w|E. E. Cummings}}|title={{w|The Enormous Room}}|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.184803|chapter=4|page=80|publisher=Modern Library|year_published=1949|location=New York
|passage=A description of the ''cour'' would be incomplete without an enumeration of the manifold duties of the ''planton'' in charge, which were as follows: to prevent the men from using the horizontal bar, except for '''chinning''', since if you swung yourself upon it you could look over the wall into the women’s ''cour'' {{...}}}}
#* '''1969''', {{w|Kurt Vonnegut}}, ''{{w|Slaughterhouse-Five}}'', New York: Dial, 2005, Chapter 5, p.{{nbsp}}119,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/slaughterhousefi00vonn_0]</sup>
#*: The Englishmen had also been lifting weights and '''chinning themselves''' for years. Their bellies were like washboards. The muscles of their calves and upper arms were like cannonballs.
#* '''1986''', Martin Cohen, ''The Marine Corps 3X Fitness Program'', Boston: Little, Brown, Part 3, p.{{nbsp}}75,<sup>[https://archive.org/stream/marinecorps3xfit00cohe]</sup>
#*: You can grunt and curse to your heart’s content but you cannot swing your body when '''chinning'''.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|UK|transitive}} To [[punch]] or [[hit]] (someone)'s chin {{gloss|part of the body}}.
#* '''1915''', {{w|Ralph Henry Barbour}}, ''Left Tackle Thayer'', New York: Dodd, Mead, Chapter 14, pp.{{nbsp}}183-184,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/lefttacklethayer00barb_0]</sup>
#*: He told me once that he used to be scared to death every time he started in a hard game for fear he’d get badly injured. Said it wasn’t until someone had jabbed him in the nose or ‘'''chinned'''’ him that he forgot to be scared.
